//science.nasa.gov/photojournal/rovers-wheel-churns-up-bright-martian-soil/","x","",0],["img/archive/mars/spirit/04-sol-1889-at-the-site-the-team-named-troy-the-haz.webp","img/archive/mars/spirit/04-sol-1889-at-the-site-the-team-named-troy-the-haz.webp","PIA12002, a black and white wide-angle view from Spirit's front hazard-avoidance camera on sol 1889, 26 April 2009, showing the soil churned up by two short backward drives that slipped, with Husband Hill on the horizon.","NASA/JPL-Caltech","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/spirit-slipping-in-soft-ground-sol-1889/","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/spirit/05-troy-the-patch-of-soft-sulfate-rich-soil-spirit.webp","img/archive/mars/spirit/05-troy-the-patch-of-soft-sulfate-rich-soil-spirit.webp","PIA12203, the Calypso Panorama: a full-circle Pancam mosaic assembled from hundreds of frames taken between sols 1906 and 1943, 14 May to 20 June 2009, from the embedded rover's position at Troy, with the west edge of Home Plate filling the right half and Husband Hill in the distance near the centre.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/Cornell University","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/calypso-panorama-of-spirits-view-from-troy/","x","",0],["img/archive/mars/spirit/06-dust-is-what-kills-a-solar-rover-spirit-s-deck-i.webp","img/archive/mars/spirit/06-dust-is-what-kills-a-solar-rover-spirit-s-deck-i.webp","PIA10128, a vertical-projection Pancam self-portrait of Spirit's deck built from frames taken over sols 1355 to 1358, 26 to 29 October 2007, showing the solar arrays under a heavy coat of dust.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/Cornell","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/dusty-solar-panels-on-spirit/","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/spirit/07-spirit-in-final-checkout-at-kennedy-space-center.webp","img/archive/mars/spirit/07-spirit-in-final-checkout-at-kennedy-space-center.webp","Technicians in cleanroom garments work on the Spirit rover during final checkout in the Payload Hazardous Servicing Facility at Kennedy Space Center on 28 March 2003, with the STS-107 memorial plaque visible mounted on the rover's high-gain antenna.","NASA/KSC (the source record names no individual photographer)","https://images.nasa.gov/details/KSC-03pd3272","u","",0]],"mars-reconnaissance-orbiter":[["img/archive/mars/mars-reconnaissance-orbiter/01-artist-s-concept-of-mro-over-nilosyrtis-an-illus.webp","img/archive/mars/mars-reconnaissance-orbiter/01-artist-s-concept-of-mro-over-nilosyrtis-an-illus.thumb.webp","An artist's concept of Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter over the Nilosyrtis region with its high-gain antenna, twin solar arrays and instrument deck facing the surface.","NASA/JPL","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/mars-reconnaissance-orbiter-over-nilosyrtis-artists-concept","a","",1],["img/archive/mars/mars-reconnaissance-orbiter/02-the-people-who-built-it-lockheed-martin-technici.webp","img/archive/mars/mars-reconnaissance-orbiter/02-the-people-who-built-it-lockheed-martin-technici.webp","Lockheed Martin technicians on work platforms prepare a full range-of-motion gimbal test of the Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter high-gain antenna in the Payload Hazardous Servicing Facility at Kennedy Space Center on 25 May 2005.","NASA/KSC","https://images.nasa.gov/details/05pd1113","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/mars-reconnaissance-orbiter/03-launch-12-august-2005-nasa-s-first-atlas-v-fligh.webp","img/archive/mars/mars-reconnaissance-orbiter/03-launch-12-august-2005-nasa-s-first-atlas-v-fligh.webp","Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter lifts off at 7:43 a.m. EDT on 12 August 2005 on a Lockheed Martin Atlas V from Launch Complex 41 at Cape Canaveral Air Force Station, NASA's first use of the Atlas V.","NASA/KSC","https://images.nasa.gov/details/05pd1869","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/mars-reconnaissance-orbiter/04-one-spacecraft-photographing-another-mid-landing.webp","img/archive/mars/mars-reconnaissance-orbiter/04-one-spacecraft-photographing-another-mid-landing.webp","HiRISE catches the Perseverance descent stage falling through the Martian atmosphere under its parachute on 18 February 2021, shot from about 700 km away while MRO was moving at roughly 3 km per second, with the Jezero delta at left.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/University of Arizona","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/hirise-captured-perseverance-during-descent-to-mars","p","",0],["img/archive/mars/mars-reconnaissance-orbiter/05-china-s-tianwen-1-platform-and-the-zhurong-rover.webp","img/archive/mars/mars-reconnaissance-orbiter/05-china-s-tianwen-1-platform-and-the-zhurong-rover.webp","HiRISE observation ESP_069665_2055, acquired 6 June 2021 at about 29 cm per pixel, showing China's Tianwen-1 landing platform as the bright spot at the centre of a dark blast pattern in southern Utopia Planitia, with the Zhurong rover as a smaller bright spot just to the south.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/UArizona","https://www.uahirise.org/ESP_069665_2055","p","",0],["img/archive/mars/mars-reconnaissance-orbiter/06-where-schiaparelli-hit-six-days-after-the-fact-t.webp","img/archive/mars/mars-reconnaissance-orbiter/06-where-schiaparelli-hit-six-days-after-the-fact-t.webp","HiRISE view of 25 October 2016 showing the ground where ESA's Schiaparelli test lander struck Meridiani Planum on 19 October 2016, with magnified insets of the three sites where separate components of the spacecraft hit.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/Univ. of Arizona","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/closer-look-at-schiaparelli-impact-site-on-mars","p","",0],["img/archive/mars/mars-reconnaissance-orbiter/07-recurring-slope-lineae-in-garni-crater-the-2015.webp","img/archive/mars/mars-reconnaissance-orbiter/07-recurring-slope-lineae-in-garni-crater-the-2015.webp","Dark narrow streaks known as recurring slope lineae running down the walls of Garni Crater, shown on a HiRISE digital terrain model with the vertical scale exaggerated, published in September 2015 when NASA presented them as evidence of present-day briny water.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/Univ. of Arizona","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/dark-recurring-streaks-on-walls-of-garni-crater","p","",0]],"phoenix":[["img/archive/mars/phoenix/01-phoenix-photographed-its-own-solar-panel-and-its.webp","img/archive/mars/phoenix/01-phoenix-photographed-its-own-solar-panel-and-its.thumb.webp","Surface Stereo Imager panorama looking out over the arctic plain, with one of the lander's circular solar panels in the foreground and the robotic arm holding a soil sample in its scoop.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/University of Arizona/Texas A&M University","https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA10793","x","",1],["img/archive/mars/phoenix/02-caught-on-the-way-down-phoenix-hanging-under-its.webp","img/archive/mars/phoenix/02-caught-on-the-way-down-phoenix-hanging-under-its.webp","HiRISE image from Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter showing Phoenix descending under its parachute on 25 May 2008, with a large crater behind it. The lander and parachute are only a few pixels across at full scale, so NASA boxed them and repeated them enlarged in an inset at the lower left.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/University of Arizona","https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA10705","p","",0],["img/archive/mars/phoenix/03-under-the-lander-on-sol-5-the-descent-engines-sw.webp","img/archive/mars/phoenix/03-under-the-lander-on-sol-5-the-descent-engines-sw.webp","Robotic Arm Camera view underneath the lander on sol 5, framed by landing struts, showing the smooth bright patch left where the descent thrusters blew the loose soil aside.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/University of Arizona/Max Planck Institute","https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA10741","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/phoenix/04-the-evidence-four-days-apart-a-cluster-of-bright.webp","img/archive/mars/phoenix/04-the-evidence-four-days-apart-a-cluster-of-bright.webp","Two Surface Stereo Imager frames of the Dodo-Goldilocks trench, labelled sol 20 and sol 24 (15 and 19 June 2008), in which a cluster of bright lumps in the lower left of the trench floor shrinks and disappears over four days. The larger bright patch higher up the trench is still there in both frames.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/University of Arizona/Texas A&M University","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phoenix_Ice.jpg","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/phoenix/05-the-snow-white-trench-after-scraping-material-wo.webp","img/archive/mars/phoenix/05-the-snow-white-trench-after-scraping-material-wo.webp","The floor of the trench informally named Snow White in July 2008, scored with rasp and scrape marks made in preparation for collecting a sample of the hard subsurface layer.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/University of Arizona/Texas A&M University","https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA10978","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/phoenix/06-snow-on-mars-recorded-as-data-the-lidar-profile.webp","img/archive/mars/phoenix/06-snow-on-mars-recorded-as-data-the-lidar-profile.webp","A lidar data plot rather than a photograph: the Canadian-built lidar on Phoenix profiling a cloud on sol 99, with labelled fall streaks of ice crystals descending from the cloud base near 4 km altitude.","NASA / JPL / UA / MET team","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lidar_Fall_Streaks.jpg","p","",0],["img/archive/mars/phoenix/07-phoenix-leaves-earth-on-a-delta-ii-7925-from-cap.webp","img/archive/mars/phoenix/07-phoenix-leaves-earth-on-a-delta-ii-7925-from-cap.webp","The Delta II 7925 carrying Phoenix lifting off from Pad 17A at Cape Canaveral Air Force Station at 5:26 a.m. EDT on 4 August 2007, with the mission emblem visible on the rocket. NASA's own record for this frame credits the photographers Regina Mitchell-Ryall and Jerry Cannon.","NASA/Regina Mitchell-Ryall and Jerry Cannon","https://images.nasa.gov/details/KSC-07pd2173","s","",0]],"phobos-grunt":[["img/archive/mars/phobos-grunt-yinghuo-1/04-an-exhibition-model-not-flight-hardware-but-the.webp","img/archive/mars/phobos-grunt-yinghuo-1/04-an-exhibition-model-not-flight-hardware-but-the.webp","A Roscosmos display model of Phobos-Grunt at the 2011 Paris Air Show, numbered by the photographer to identify the drop tank, the cruise module, Yinghuo-1, the orbiter and lander, the Earth re-entry module and the sample capsule.","Pline, CC BY-SA 3.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Model_of_Phobos-Grunt_spacecraft_2011_P1110983.jpg","p","CC BY-SA 3.0",0],["img/archive/mars/phobos-grunt-yinghuo-1/05-phobos-grunt-seen-from-cornwall-on-27-november-2.webp","img/archive/mars/phobos-grunt-yinghuo-1/05-phobos-grunt-seen-from-cornwall-on-27-november-2.webp","A half-second handheld exposure taken from Cornwall on the evening of 27 November 2011 showing Phobos-Grunt as a streak of light crossing Cygnus while stranded in low Earth orbit.","Nathanial Bradford, CC BY 2.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%A4%D0%BE%D0%B1%D0%BE%D1%81-%D0%93%D1%80%D1%83%D0%BD%D1%82_Phobos-Grunt_from_Cornwall_(6412959879).jpg","u","CC BY 2.0",0],["img/archive/mars/phobos-grunt-yinghuo-1/06-the-dish-that-heard-it-esa-modified-the-feed-on.webp","img/archive/mars/phobos-grunt-yinghuo-1/06-the-dish-that-heard-it-esa-modified-the-feed-on.webp","ESA's tracking station at Perth in Western Australia, showing the 15 metre main dish and the small secondary antenna above it that was used to command Phobos-Grunt.","ESA, via RussianSpaceWeb.com","https://www.russianspaceweb.com/phobos_grunt_launch.html","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/phobos-grunt-yinghuo-1/07-where-it-was-going-esa-s-mars-express-imaged-pho.webp","img/archive/mars/phobos-grunt-yinghuo-1/07-where-it-was-going-esa-s-mars-express-imaged-pho.webp","A Mars Express High Resolution Stereo Camera image of Phobos at 8.2 metres per pixel from orbit 8974, with two ellipses drawn on it marking the earlier planned landing site in red and the site under consideration in blue for Phobos-Grunt.","ESA/DLR/FU Berlin (G. Neukum), CC BY-SA 3.0 IGO","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Planned_landing_site_of_the_Russian_Phobos-Grunt_mission_ESA235191.jpg","u","CC BY-SA 3.0",0]],"curiosity":[["img/archive/mars/curiosity/01-curiosity-on-vera-rubin-ridge-on-sol-1943-with-m.webp","img/archive/mars/curiosity/01-curiosity-on-vera-rubin-ridge-on-sol-1943-with-m.thumb.webp","PIA22207, a self-portrait of Curiosity standing on Vera Rubin Ridge, assembled from dozens of Mars Hand Lens Imager frames all taken on 23 January 2018, sol 1943, with the lower slopes of Aeolis Mons rising just behind the rover's mast and the rim of Gale crater on the horizon at both edges.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/MSSS","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/mount-sharp-photobombs-curiosity/","u","",1],["img/archive/mars/curiosity/02-curiosity-photographed-on-the-way-down-by-anothe.webp","img/archive/mars/curiosity/02-curiosity-photographed-on-the-way-down-by-anothe.webp","PIA15978, the HiRISE camera on Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter catching the Mars Science Laboratory entry vehicle descending under its fully inflated parachute on 6 August 2012 UTC, with an inset cutout of the back shell stretched to avoid saturation.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/Univ. of Arizona","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/curiosity-spotted-on-parachute-by-orbiter/","p","",0],["img/archive/mars/curiosity/03-the-atlas-v-541-leaving-complex-41-on-26-novembe.webp","img/archive/mars/curiosity/03-the-atlas-v-541-leaving-complex-41-on-26-novembe.webp","The United Launch Alliance Atlas V 541 rising from Space Launch Complex 41 at Cape Canaveral on 26 November 2011 with the Mars Science Laboratory spacecraft sealed inside its payload fairing. NASA's Kennedy Space Center media archive is the source; the file credits NASA without naming an individual photographer.","NASA","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mars_Science_Laboratory_Launch.jpg","p","",0],["img/archive/mars/curiosity/04-the-hole-in-john-klein-the-mudstone-slab-curiosi.webp","img/archive/mars/curiosity/04-the-hole-in-john-klein-the-mudstone-slab-curiosi.webp","PIA16726, a Mars Hand Lens Imager view centred on the borehole Curiosity's drill cut into the mudstone slab called John Klein in Yellowknife Bay, the mission's first sample drilling on Mars.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/MSSS","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/curiositys-first-sample-drilling/","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/curiosity/05-yellowknife-bay-the-low-spot-curiosity-reached-f.webp","img/archive/mars/curiosity/05-yellowknife-bay-the-low-spot-curiosity-reached-f.webp","PIA17595, a Curiosity mosaic showing the stacked geological members of the Yellowknife Bay formation and marking the two places where the rover drilled into the lowest member, Sheepbed, at the targets John Klein and Cumberland.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/MSSS","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/view-of-yellowknife-bay-formation-with-drilling-sites/","x","",0],["img/archive/mars/curiosity/06-curiosity-s-left-middle-and-left-rear-wheels-on.webp","img/archive/mars/curiosity/06-curiosity-s-left-middle-and-left-rear-wheels-on.webp","PIA20334, a Mars Hand Lens Imager view of Curiosity's left middle and left rear wheels, part of a routine wheel inspection set taken on 18 April 2016, sol 1315, showing punctures and tears in the aluminium skin.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/MSSS","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/routine-inspection-of-rover-wheel-wear-and-tear/","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/curiosity/07-curiosity-at-the-mojave-site-on-lower-mount-shar.webp","img/archive/mars/curiosity/07-curiosity-at-the-mojave-site-on-lower-mount-shar.webp","PIA19142, a Curiosity self-portrait at the Mojave site on lower Aeolis Mons, combining dozens of Mars Hand Lens Imager frames taken on sols 868, 882 and 884 in January 2015, where the drill collected the mission's second sample of the mountain.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/MSSS","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/curiosity-self-portrait-at-mojave-site-on-mount-sharp/","u","",0]],"mangalyaan":[["img/archive/mars/mangalyaan/01-the-whole-planet-in-one-frame-mangalyaan-s-high.webp","img/archive/mars/mangalyaan/01-the-whole-planet-in-one-frame-mangalyaan-s-high.thumb.webp","True-colour view of the whole Martian disc built from Mars Colour Camera frames taken on 10 October 2014 from about 76,000 km, showing the Tharsis volcanoes and Valles Marineris, processed by Justin Cowart from ISRO's archived data.","ISRO / ISSDC / Justin Cowart, CC BY 2.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tharsis_and_Valles_Marineris_-_Mars_Orbiter_Mission_(30055660701).png","u","CC BY 2.0",1]],"maven":[["img/archive/mars/maven/01-maven-s-solar-arrays-being-test-deployed-at-kenn.webp","img/archive/mars/maven/01-maven-s-solar-arrays-being-test-deployed-at-kenn.thumb.webp","Engineers and technicians test deploy MAVEN's twin solar arrays inside the Payload Hazardous Servicing Facility at NASA's Kennedy Space Center on 23 September 2013, about eight weeks before launch.","NASA/Kim Shiflett","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MAVEN_in_KSC%27s_PHSF_preparing_for_launch_(KSC-2013-3634).jpg","u","",1],["img/archive/mars/maven/02-launch-18-november-2013-the-atlas-v-lifting-mave.webp","img/archive/mars/maven/02-launch-18-november-2013-the-atlas-v-lifting-mave.webp","The United Launch Alliance Atlas V carrying MAVEN lifts off from Space Launch Complex 41 at Cape Canaveral on 18 November 2013.","NASA/Bill Ingalls","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atlas_V_launches_with_MAVEN_(201311180003HQ).jpg","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/maven/03-maven-s-first-look-eight-hours-after-arrival-hyd.webp","img/archive/mars/maven/03-maven-s-first-look-eight-hours-after-arrival-hyd.webp","False colour ultraviolet images of Mars in three wavelength bands, taken by MAVEN's Imaging Ultraviolet Spectrograph from an altitude of 36,500 km eight hours after orbit insertion in September 2014. Blue shows sunlight scattered by the extended cloud of atomic hydrogen escaping the planet.","NASA/Goddard Space Flight Center","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MAVEN_Spacecraft_Returns_First_Mars_Observations_(15164912327).png","x","",0]],"exomars-2016":[["img/archive/mars/exomars-tgo/01-real-hardware-twelve-days-before-launch-tgo-and.webp","img/archive/mars/exomars-tgo/01-real-hardware-twelve-days-before-launch-tgo-and.thumb.webp","The assembled ExoMars 2016 spacecraft composite, the Trace Gas Orbiter with the Schiaparelli demonstrator mounted on it, photographed on 2 March 2016 at the Baikonur cosmodrome as the second half of the Proton launcher fairing is lowered around it by overhead crane.","ESA / B. Bethge","https://www.esa.int/ESA_Multimedia/Images/2016/03/Encapsulating_ExoMars_2016","x","",1],["img/archive/mars/schiaparelli/01-six-days-after-the-crash-hirise-picks-out-the-im.webp","img/archive/mars/schiaparelli/01-six-days-after-the-crash-hirise-picks-out-the-im.thumb.webp","HiRISE view taken on 25 October 2016, six days after the crash, of the ground in Meridiani Planum where Schiaparelli came down, with magnified insets of three places where parts of the spacecraft reached the surface: the lander module's dark impact scar, the parachute with the back shell, and the front heat shield.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/Univ. of Arizona","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PIA21131_Closer_Look_at_Schiaparelli_Impact_Site_on_Mars.jpg","p","",1],["img/archive/mars/exomars-tgo/02-the-orbiter-s-backbone-tgo-s-central-cylinder-du.webp","img/archive/mars/exomars-tgo/02-the-orbiter-s-backbone-tgo-s-central-cylinder-du.webp","The 1.194 m diameter, 3.5 m high composite central cylinder that carries launch loads through the ExoMars Trace Gas Orbiter and houses its propellant and oxidiser tanks, seen during integration of the spacecraft's electrical subsystems in the Thales Alenia Space cleanroom at Cannes, France.","ESA / Anneke Le Floc'h","https://www.esa.int/ESA_Multimedia/Images/2015/01/ExoMars_Trace_Gas_Orbiter","x","",0],["img/archive/mars/exomars-tgo/03-what-tgo-actually-looks-at-frost-edged-polygons.webp","img/archive/mars/exomars-tgo/03-what-tgo-actually-looks-at-frost-edged-polygons.webp","Frost-fringed polygonal ground on a springtime morning in the southern mid-latitudes of Mars near Dorsa Brevia, captured on 21 June 2020 by the CaSSIS colour and stereo camera on the Trace Gas Orbiter.","ESA/TGO/CaSSIS, CC BY-SA 3.0 IGO","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ExoMars_TGO_view_of_frost-fringed_polygons_ESA502025.jpg","u","CC BY-SA 3.0",0],["img/archive/mars/schiaparelli/03-in-colour-on-1-november-2016-the-lander-scar-the.webp","img/archive/mars/schiaparelli/03-in-colour-on-1-november-2016-the-lander-scar-the.webp","The first colour HiRISE view of the Schiaparelli impact site, observed on 1 November 2016, presented as three cutouts: the lander module at top, the parachute and back shell at lower left, and the heat shield at lower right, which fell outside the colour strip and so appears without colour.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/Univ. of Arizona","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schiaparelli_site.jpg","p","",0]],"insight":[["img/archive/mars/insight/01-insight-ten-sols-after-landing-photographed-by-t.webp","img/archive/mars/insight/01-insight-ten-sols-after-landing-photographed-by-t.thumb.webp","InSight's first full selfie, a mosaic of 11 frames taken by the Instrument Deployment Camera on the elbow of the robotic arm on 6 December 2018, showing the solar panels, the deck, the weather sensor booms and the UHF antenna.","NASA/JPL-Caltech","https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA22876","x","",1],["img/archive/mars/insight-mole/01-hp3-under-test-in-a-german-cleanroom-before-laun.webp","img/archive/mars/insight-mole/01-hp3-under-test-in-a-german-cleanroom-before-laun.thumb.webp","The Heat Flow and Physical Properties Package standing on a bench in a cleanroom in Germany before launch, released by JPL as PIA23044 with the caption that the heat probe was being tested in Germany. JPL does not state whether the unit shown is the flight model. The black upright tube is the support structure that holds the mole; the horizontal box beside it carries the tether compartments, and a separate electronics box sits on a cloth at the right. Yellow ESD tape reading ESD GESCHÜTZTER BEREICH runs across the foreground.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/DLR","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/hp3-being-tested-in-germany/","u","",1],["img/archive/mars/insight/02-into-the-fog-at-vandenberg-5-may-2018-the-first.webp","img/archive/mars/insight/02-into-the-fog-at-vandenberg-5-may-2018-the-first.webp","The Atlas V 401 carrying InSight and the two MarCO CubeSats climbing into thick fog from Space Launch Complex 3 at Vandenberg Air Force Base before dawn on 5 May 2018.","NASA/Bill Ingalls","https://images.nasa.gov/details/NHQ201805050003","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/insight/03-19-december-2018-seis-on-the-ground-the-viking-s.webp","img/archive/mars/insight/03-19-december-2018-seis-on-the-ground-the-viking-s.webp","The copper-coloured SEIS seismometer standing on the Martian surface near dusk on 19 December 2018, photographed by the camera on InSight's robotic arm shortly after the arm set it down.","NASA/JPL-Caltech","https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA22956","p","",0],["img/archive/mars/insight/04-the-shield-going-down-over-the-seismometer-on-2.webp","img/archive/mars/insight/04-the-shield-going-down-over-the-seismometer-on-2.webp","The dome-shaped Wind and Thermal Shield in place over the seismometer after InSight lowered it on 2 February 2019, sol 66.","NASA/JPL-Caltech","https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA22959","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/insight/05-the-end-of-the-mole-9-january-2021-after-a-last.webp","img/archive/mars/insight/05-the-end-of-the-mole-9-january-2021-after-a-last.webp","The pit around the HP3 mole as it looked after hammering on 9 January 2021, sol 754, with the scoop on the robotic arm above it and the copper science tether running away to the left.","NASA/JPL-Caltech","https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA24263","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/insight/06-the-last-selfie-24-april-2022-set-it-beside-the.webp","img/archive/mars/insight/06-the-last-selfie-24-april-2022-set-it-beside-the.webp","InSight's final selfie, a mosaic assembled from images taken on 24 April 2022, sol 1211, with the solar panels and deck buried under a thick coat of Martian dust.","NASA/JPL-Caltech","https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA25287","x","",0],["img/archive/mars/insight/07-11-december-2022-one-of-the-last-pictures-it-sen.webp","img/archive/mars/insight/07-11-december-2022-one-of-the-last-pictures-it-sen.webp","One of the last images InSight returned, taken on 11 December 2022, sol 1436, showing the seismometer under its shield on the ground in front of the lander.","NASA/JPL-Caltech","https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA25680","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/insight-mole/02-how-the-parts-fit-together-including-the-tether.webp","img/archive/mars/insight-mole/02-how-the-parts-fit-together-including-the-tether.webp","A labelled rendering of the whole HP3 instrument, published by JPL under the title HP3 Cutaway Rendering (Artist's Concept). It annotates the grapple point, the tether length monitor, the tether compartments, the engineering tether, the feet, the connectors box, the orange science tether embedded with passive temperature sensors, and the mole itself, described as carrying the hammering mechanism, heaters and sensors for thermal conductivity, and static tilt sensors.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/DLR","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/hp3-cutaway-rendering-artists-concept/","a","",0],["img/archive/mars/insight-mole/03-hp3-on-the-ground-at-homestead-hollow-in-februar.webp","img/archive/mars/insight-mole/03-hp3-on-the-ground-at-homestead-hollow-in-februar.webp","A frame from InSight's Instrument Deployment Camera showing the HP3 support structure standing on the ground of Elysium Planitia with the robotic arm's grapple directly above it and the engineering tether looping back towards the lander. JPL's caption states that InSight set the heat probe on the Martian surface on 12 February 2019.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/DLR","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/hp3-on-the-martian-surface/","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/insight-mole/04-sol-325-the-pale-rod-at-the-left-is-the-mole-ham.webp","img/archive/mars/insight-mole/04-sol-325-the-pale-rod-at-the-left-is-the-mole-ham.webp","An Instrument Deployment Camera frame from 26 October 2019, Sol 325, published by JPL with the caption that the mole is seen after backing about halfway out of the hole it had burrowed. The pale mole body lies tilted at upper left, half out of the ground, with the dark scoop of the robotic arm set into the pit beside it and the science tether running off to the left.","NASA/JPL-Caltech","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/insights-heat-probe-partially-backs-out-of-hole/","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/insight-mole/05-june-2020-the-back-cap-push-campaign-the-scoop-i.webp","img/archive/mars/insight-mole/05-june-2020-the-back-cap-push-campaign-the-scoop-i.webp","An Instrument Deployment Camera frame from 20 June 2020 showing the scoop on the end of InSight's robotic arm pressed down into the mole's pit, holding the mole in the ground. JPL's caption records that the movement of sand grains inside the scoop suggested the mole was bumping up against the bottom of the scoop while hammering.","NASA/JPL-Caltech","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/nasa-insights-mole-taps-the-bottom-of-the-landers-scoop/","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/insight-mole/06-the-free-mole-test-9-january-2021-five-hundred-s.webp","img/archive/mars/insight-mole/06-the-free-mole-test-9-january-2021-five-hundred-s.webp","An Instrument Deployment Camera frame from 9 January 2021, Sol 754, published by JPL as the mole after its final hammering session. The scoop is pressed into the loose regolith that had been scraped over the buried mole, the science tether runs away to the left, and the disturbed ground of the pit is visible around it. JPL's caption states that with no progress the team called an end to their efforts that day.","NASA/JPL-Caltech","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/insights-mole-comes-to-rest/","u","",0]],"marco":[["img/archive/mars/marco-a-b/01-both-marco-cubesats-before-launch-one-folded-and.webp","img/archive/mars/marco-a-b/01-both-marco-cubesats-before-launch-one-folded-and.thumb.webp","Engineer Joel Steinkraus with both MarCO spacecraft at the Jet Propulsion Laboratory in March 2018. The unit on the left is folded as it flew stowed on the rocket; the one on the right has its solar panels out, with its flat reflectarray high-gain antenna standing up behind it.","NASA/JPL-Caltech","https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA22319","u","",1],["img/archive/mars/marco-a-b/02-wall-e-s-farewell-marco-b-photographed-mars-on-t.webp","img/archive/mars/marco-a-b/02-wall-e-s-farewell-marco-b-photographed-mars-on-t.webp","Mars photographed by MarCO-B's wide-angle camera on 26 November 2018 as the CubeSat flew away after InSight landed, with the grid of its reflectarray antenna along the right edge of the frame. The picture sits letterboxed inside a 1920 by 1080 canvas.","NASA/JPL-Caltech","https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA22833","p","",0],["img/archive/mars/marco-a-b/03-the-first-picture-of-mars-ever-returned-by-a-cub.webp","img/archive/mars/marco-a-b/03-the-first-picture-of-mars-ever-returned-by-a-cub.webp","NASA's annotated version of MarCO-B's wide-angle camera view, taken as a test of exposure settings on 2 October 2018. Mars is the small red dot right of centre, ringed with a dashed circle and labelled by NASA; parts of the CubeSat's unfolded high-gain antenna show on both sides of the frame.","NASA/JPL-Caltech","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/first-image-of-mars-from-a-cubesat/","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/marco-a-b/04-earth-and-the-moon-seen-from-a-briefcase-days-af.webp","img/archive/mars/marco-a-b/04-earth-and-the-moon-seen-from-a-briefcase-days-af.webp","The first image returned by MarCO-B after launch, showing its unfolded high-gain antenna at right and Earth with the Moon as two points of light near the centre.","NASA/JPL-Caltech","https://images.nasa.gov/details/PIA22323","u","",0]],"hope":[["img/archive/mars/hope-emm/02-orbit-47-1-may-2021-from-21-519-km-up-this-is-wh.webp","img/archive/mars/hope-emm/02-orbit-47-1-may-2021-from-21-519-km-up-this-is-wh.webp","The full disc of Mars assembled from Hope EXI red, green and blue filter frames taken on 1 May 2021 during orbit 47, from a spacecraft altitude of 21,519 km, processed by Andrea Luck.","UAESA/MBRSC/HopeMarsMission/EXI/AndreaLuck, CC BY 2.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mars_-_Orbit_47_-_Hope_Mission_(51584045741).png","u","CC BY 2.0",0],["img/archive/mars/hope-emm/04-omran-sharaf-announcing-the-science-mission-in-d.webp","img/archive/mars/hope-emm/04-omran-sharaf-announcing-the-science-mission-in-d.webp","Omran Sharaf, project manager of the Emirates Mars Mission, speaking at the official announcement of the mission's scientific objectives at Al Bahr Palace in Dubai on 6 May 2015, in front of Arabic text reading 'First Arab probe to explore Mars'.","Abraham Que, CC BY-SA 4.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Omran_Sharaf_at_Hope_Scientific_Mission_Announcement_Event.jpg","u","CC BY-SA 4.0",0],["img/archive/mars/hope-emm/05-hope-arrives-in-japan-late-april-2020-flown-in-f.webp","img/archive/mars/hope-emm/05-hope-arrives-in-japan-late-april-2020-flown-in-f.webp","The sealed shipping container carrying the Hope spacecraft being lifted from a Volga-Dnepr Antonov freighter in Japan in late April 2020, after the flight from Dubai. The spacecraft itself is inside the container and not visible; the container carries the mission decal.","Mohammed msx, CC BY-SA 4.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%B1%D9%83%D8%A8%D8%A9_%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D8%B6%D8%A7%D8%A6%D9%8A%D8%A9_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%B1%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A9_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A5%D9%85%D8%A7%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%AA%D9%8A%D8%A9_%28%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%85%D9%84%29.jpg","u","CC BY-SA 4.0",0],["img/archive/mars/hope-emm/06-a-full-size-mockup-of-hope-photographed-at-the-d.webp","img/archive/mars/hope-emm/06-a-full-size-mockup-of-hope-photographed-at-the-d.webp","A full-size mockup of the Hope orbiter displayed at the International Astronautical Congress in Dubai in October 2021, showing the hexagonal bus, the two solar wings and the high-gain antenna.","BugWarp, CC BY-SA 4.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emirates_Mars_Mission_mockup_at_IAC_2021_01_(cropped).jpg","u","CC BY-SA 4.0",0]],"perseverance":[["img/archive/mars/ingenuity/01-ingenuity-alone-on-mars-on-19-april-2021-photogr.webp","img/archive/mars/ingenuity/01-ingenuity-alone-on-mars-on-19-april-2021-photogr.thumb.webp","PIA24550, a Mastcam-Z mosaic taken on 19 April 2021 after Ingenuity's first flight, showing the helicopter standing on its four legs on the floor of Jezero crater at Wright Brothers Field, with rover wheel tracks crossing the foreground.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/ASU/MSSS","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/ingenuitys-first-flight-recorded-by-mastcam-z/","x","",1],["img/archive/mars/perseverance/01-perseverance-and-cheyava-falls-the-pale-arrowhea.webp","img/archive/mars/perseverance/01-perseverance-and-cheyava-falls-the-pale-arrowhea.thumb.webp","PIA26344, a Perseverance self-portrait made from 62 WATSON frames taken on 23 July 2024, with the arrowhead-shaped rock nicknamed Cheyava Falls lying to the left of the rover near the centre of the picture.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/MSSS","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/perseverances-selfie-with-cheyava-falls/","u","",1],["img/archive/mars/three-forks-depot/01-the-whole-depot-one-tube-at-a-time-each-strip-is.webp","img/archive/mars/three-forks-depot/01-the-whole-depot-one-tube-at-a-time-each-strip-is.thumb.webp","PIA25738, a photomontage of ten WATSON images, each showing one sealed titanium sample tube lying on the Martian surface shortly after Perseverance set it down at Three Forks between 21 December 2022 and 28 January 2023. From the left: Malay, Mageik, Crosswind Lake, Roubion, Coulettes, Montdenier, Bearwallow, Skyland, Atsah and Amalik.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/MSSS","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/watsons-photomontage-of-mars-sample-depot/","u","",1],["img/archive/mars/ingenuity/02-the-first-flight-seen-from-the-aircraft-ingenuit.webp","img/archive/mars/ingenuity/02-the-first-flight-seen-from-the-aircraft-ingenuit.webp","PIA24584, the image Ingenuity's downward-looking black-and-white navigation camera took of its own shadow while hovering above Jezero crater on 19 April 2021, during the first instance of powered, controlled flight on another planet.","NASA/JPL-Caltech","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/ingenuitys-first-black-and-white-image-from-the-air/","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/ingenuity/03-ingenuity-at-airfield-b-on-7-may-2021-at-the-end.webp","img/archive/mars/ingenuity/03-ingenuity-at-airfield-b-on-7-may-2021-at-the-end.webp","PIA24648, a Mastcam-Z view of Ingenuity standing at its new landing site after Flight 5 on 7 May 2021, a one-way trip of 129 m to a height record of 10 m that lasted 108 seconds.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/ASU/MSSS","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/mastcam-z-views-ingenuity-after-fifth-flight/","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/ingenuity/04-the-picture-that-ended-the-mission-looking-at-th.webp","img/archive/mars/ingenuity/04-the-picture-that-ended-the-mission-looking-at-th.webp","PIA26243, a colour image from Ingenuity's horizon-facing camera showing the shadow of one of its rotor blades cast on the sand after Flight 72 on 18 January 2024, with a clear step in the silhouette where the outer part of the blade is missing.","NASA/JPL-Caltech","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/ingenuity-spots-the-shadow-of-its-damaged-rotor-blade/","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/ingenuity/05-ingenuity-where-it-stopped-photographed-by-perse.webp","img/archive/mars/ingenuity/05-ingenuity-where-it-stopped-photographed-by-perse.webp","PIA26236, an enhanced-colour mosaic of six Mastcam-Z frames taken on 4 February 2024 from about 450 m away, showing Ingenuity where it came to rest at the spot the team named Valinor Hills after its 72nd and last flight.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/ASU/MSSS","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/perseverance-spots-ingenuity-at-its-final-airfield/","x","",0],["img/archive/mars/ingenuity/06-how-jpl-thinks-it-happened-this-is-a-diagram-not.webp","img/archive/mars/ingenuity/06-how-jpl-thinks-it-happened-this-is-a-diagram-not.webp","PIA26482, a NASA graphic published in December 2024 depicting the most likely sequence for Ingenuity's hard landing on Flight 72: high horizontal velocity at touchdown on a sloping sand ripple, causing the helicopter to pitch and roll and the blades to fail.","NASA/JPL-Caltech","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/ingenuitys-hard-landing/","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/ingenuity/07-the-ingenuity-team-at-jpl-reading-the-data-from.webp","img/archive/mars/ingenuity/07-the-ingenuity-team-at-jpl-reading-the-data-from.webp","PIA24597, members of the Ingenuity team in the control room at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory reacting to the data showing the helicopter had completed its second flight on Mars, on 22 April 2021.","NASA/JPL-Caltech","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/ingenuitys-team-reacts-to-data-showing-helicopter-completed-its-second-flight/","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/perseverance/02-perseverance-hanging-under-the-sky-crane-seconds.webp","img/archive/mars/perseverance/02-perseverance-hanging-under-the-sky-crane-seconds.webp","PIA24428, a still frame from video shot by a downward-looking camera on the descent stage as Perseverance was lowered on the sky crane's cables toward the floor of Jezero crater on 18 February 2021, with dust raised by the descent engines around the rover.","NASA/JPL-Caltech","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/high-resolution-still-image-of-perseverances-landing/","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/perseverance/03-launch-30-july-2020-the-ingenuity-helicopter-was.webp","img/archive/mars/perseverance/03-launch-30-july-2020-the-ingenuity-helicopter-was.webp","The United Launch Alliance Atlas V 541 lifting off from Space Launch Complex 41 at Cape Canaveral Air Force Station on 30 July 2020 at 7:50 a.m. EDT, carrying Perseverance and the Ingenuity helicopter, seen from the Atlas Space Operations Center.","NASA/Frank Michaux","https://images.nasa.gov/details/KSC-20200730-PH-FMX01_0042","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/perseverance/04-the-jezero-delta-the-ancient-river-deposit-perse.webp","img/archive/mars/perseverance/04-the-jezero-delta-the-ancient-river-deposit-perse.webp","PIA25212, a Mastcam-Z panorama of 64 frames taken on 11 April 2022, sol 406, looking out across the fan-shaped river delta on the western side of Jezero crater, with sand bedforms running along its base. NASA states that the colour bands have been processed to accentuate differences and that the sky would not look blue to a human standing there.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/ASU/MSSS","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/jezero-craters-delta-is-getting-closer/","x","",0],["img/archive/mars/perseverance/05-moxie-gold-plated-and-roughly-the-size-of-a-car.webp","img/archive/mars/perseverance/05-moxie-gold-plated-and-roughly-the-size-of-a-car.webp","PIA24203, the gold-plated Mars Oxygen In-Situ Resource Utilization Experiment photographed after installation inside Perseverance's body before launch, with the wide intake tube for filtered Martian carbon dioxide on its top surface.","NASA/JPL-Caltech","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/moxie-all-tucked-in/","p","",0],["img/archive/mars/perseverance/06-mastcam-z-looking-into-the-drill-on-4-september.webp","img/archive/mars/perseverance/06-mastcam-z-looking-into-the-drill-on-4-september.webp","PIA24832, a Mastcam-Z image taken on 4 September 2021 looking into the drill to confirm that a rock core had been retained inside the sample tube held there, the core later sealed as the sample Montdenier.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/ASU/MSSS","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/confirmation-of-montdenier-sample-in-perseverances-drill/","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/perseverance/07-the-mast-going-on-at-jpl-on-5-june-2019-fourteen.webp","img/archive/mars/perseverance/07-the-mast-going-on-at-jpl-on-5-june-2019-fourteen.webp","PIA23268, engineers and technicians installing the remote sensing mast on the Mars 2020 rover in High Bay 1 of the Spacecraft Assembly Facility at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory on 5 June 2019.","NASA/JPL-Caltech","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/mars-2020-mast-goes-on/","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/three-forks-depot/02-the-last-tube-down-28-january-2023-this-one-is-a.webp","img/archive/mars/three-forks-depot/02-the-last-tube-down-28-january-2023-this-one-is-a.webp","PIA25340, the WATSON camera on Perseverance's robotic arm photographing the tenth and final tube of the Three Forks depot on the ground on 28 January 2023, sol 690. It is the witness tube sealed at the Amalik sampling site, the only witness tube placed in the depot.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/MSSS","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/watson-documents-final-tube-dropped-at-three-forks-sample-depot/","p","",0],["img/archive/mars/three-forks-depot/03-the-finished-depot-on-31-january-2023-three-days.webp","img/archive/mars/three-forks-depot/03-the-finished-depot-on-31-january-2023-three-days.webp","PIA25736, Perseverance's Mastcam-Z portrait of the finished sample depot taken on 31 January 2023, sol 693, stitched from 368 individual frames, with the colour adjusted to approximate what the surface would look like to the human eye. The full photojournal original is 29705 by 20033 pixels and about 77 MB; this is NASA's own smaller rendition of the same image.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/ASU/MSSS","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/perseverances-portrait-of-the-sample-depot/","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/three-forks-depot/04-the-shape-of-the-depot-mapped-the-zigzag-was-not.webp","img/archive/mars/three-forks-depot/04-the-shape-of-the-depot-mapped-the-zigzag-was-not.webp","PIA25682, NASA's map of the Three Forks depot, with a labelled circle marking where each of the ten tubes was deployed in the zigzag pattern, the tubes set roughly 5 to 15 m apart so a recovery vehicle could reach them safely.","NASA/JPL-Caltech","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/perseverances-three-forks-sample-depot-map/","u","",0],["img/archive/mars/three-forks-depot/05-the-first-one-down-21-december-2022-malay-is-an.webp","img/archive/mars/three-forks-depot/05-the-first-one-down-21-december-2022-malay-is-an.webp","PIA25663, a composite of stitched WATSON frames showing the first sample tube Perseverance deposited on the Martian surface on 21 December 2022, sol 653, the igneous core informally named Malay.","NASA/JPL-Caltech/MSSS","https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/watson-documents-first-sample-on-the-martian-surface/","x","",0]],"tianwen-1":[["img/archive/mars/tianwen-1/01-tianwen-1-photographed-by-a-camera-it-threw-away.webp","img/archive/mars/tianwen-1/01-tianwen-1-photographed-by-a-camera-it-threw-away.thumb.webp","The Tianwen-1 orbiter photographed in Mars orbit by a small camera the spacecraft released, showing the gold-foiled bus, both solar wings, the high-gain antenna and the subsurface radar booms, with Mars filling the frame behind it and the bright north polar cap at upper left.","CNSA / China Planetary Exploration Program; via Ling Xin, National Science Review (article CC BY 4.0)","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tianwen-1_in_Mars_orbit.jpg","p","CC BY 4.0",1],["img/archive/mars/zhurong/01-the-portrait-zhurong-took-of-itself-the-rover-ca.webp","img/archive/mars/zhurong/01-the-portrait-zhurong-took-of-itself-the-rover-ca.thumb.webp","Zhurong and the Tianwen-1 landing platform standing side by side on the surface of southern Utopia Planitia, photographed by a small wireless camera that the rover set down on the ground and then drove away from.","中国新闻网 (China News Service), CC BY 4.0; original imagery CNSA / China Lunar Exploration Program","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zhurong-with-lander-selfie.png","p","CC BY 4.0",1],["img/archive/mars/tianwen-1/02-long-march-5-y4-leaves-wenchang-on-23-july-2020.webp","img/archive/mars/tianwen-1/02-long-march-5-y4-leaves-wenchang-on-23-july-2020.webp","A Long March 5 Y4 rocket lifting off from Launch Complex 101 at the Wenchang Space Launch Site on Hainan at 04:41:15 UT on 23 July 2020, carrying the five-tonne Tianwen-1 stack.","中新网 (China News Service), CC BY 4.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tianwen-1_launch_02.jpg","u","CC BY 4.0",0],["img/archive/mars/tianwen-1/03-an-american-orbiter-s-view-of-the-whole-architec.webp","img/archive/mars/tianwen-1/03-an-american-orbiter-s-view-of-the-whole-architec.webp","HiRISE image of the Tianwen-1 landing site in southern Utopia Planitia acquired on 6 June 2021, showing the landing platform surrounded by the bright blast pattern of its descent engines and the Zhurong rover a short distance to the south.","NASA/JPL/University of Arizona","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tianwen-1_Lander_and_Zhurong_Rover_in_Southern_Utopia_Planitia.jpg","p","",0],["img/archive/mars/tianwen-1/04-the-whole-planet-at-76-metres-per-pixel-built-fr.webp","img/archive/mars/tianwen-1/04-the-whole-planet-at-76-metres-per-pixel-built-fr.webp","China's first global colour image map of Mars, at 76 metres per pixel, assembled from Tianwen-1 Moderate Resolution Imaging Camera coverage and shown here in Robinson projection with a graticule and scale bar.","Jianjun Liu, Xin Ren, Wei Yan et al., Science Bulletin, CC BY 4.0; data CNSA / Tianwen-1 Ground Research and Application System","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1-s2.0-S2095927324002949-gr1_lrg.jpg","u","CC BY 4.0",0],["img/archive/mars/tianwen-1/05-olympus-mons-with-cloud-piling-against-it-from-s.webp","img/archive/mars/tianwen-1/05-olympus-mons-with-cloud-piling-against-it-from-s.webp","Olympus Mons under water-ice cloud, made from seven Tianwen-1 Moderate Resolution Imaging Camera frames taken on 30 December 2021 from 627 km altitude, mosaicked and reprojected by Andrea Luck from data released through moon.bao.ac.cn.","Raw image CNSA/CLEP/PEC/MoRIC; image processing Andrea Luck, CC BY 2.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mars_-_Cloudy_Olympus_Mons_-_CNSA_Tianwen-1_(54344301448).jpg","x","CC BY 2.0",0],["img/archive/mars/tianwen-1/06-the-landing-platform-photographed-by-the-rover-t.webp","img/archive/mars/tianwen-1/06-the-landing-platform-photographed-by-the-rover-t.webp","The Tianwen-1 landing platform standing on its four legs in southern Utopia Planitia with its deployment ramp lowered and the Chinese flag on its side, photographed by the Zhurong rover after it drove off on 22 May 2021.","CNSA / China Planetary Exploration Program; via Huang, X., Xu, C., Hu, J. et al., Astrodynamics (article CC BY 4.0)","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tianwen-1_lander_on_Mars.jpg","p","CC BY 4.0",0],["img/archive/mars/tianwen-1/07-where-the-orbiter-s-seven-instruments-live-this.webp","img/archive/mars/tianwen-1/07-where-the-orbiter-s-seven-instruments-live-this.webp","A labelled engineering diagram of the Tianwen-1 orbiter, drawn from the CAD model and shown from two angles, marking where its science instruments sit: the high- and medium-resolution cameras (HiRIC, MoRIC), the subsurface radar (MOSIR), the mineralogy spectrometer (MMS), the magnetometer (MOMAG) and the particle analysers (MINPA, MEPA).","Li, C., Zhang, R., Yu, D. et al., Space Science Reviews, CC BY 4.0","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tianwen-1_orbiter_payload.jpg","u","CC BY 4.0",0],["img/archive/mars/zhurong/02-zhurong-close-up-cropped-from-the-same-deployabl.webp","img/archive/mars/zhurong/02-zhurong-close-up-cropped-from-the-same-deployabl.webp","A crop of the deployable-camera portrait showing the Zhurong rover alone: the butterfly-wing solar arrays, the mast with the navigation and terrain cameras, the directional antenna and the six wheels.","中国新闻网 (China News Service), CC BY 4.0; original imagery CNSA / China Lunar Exploration Program","https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zhurong_rover_3x2_crop.png"